    <semantic:process id="_a6fa042c-9fc1-4063-a896-6a2514f4a7ca" name="Page 1" triso:readOnly="false" triso:collectEnds="true" isClosed="false">
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_b70e6306-b0fd-4928-bc6a-69e7913e655d">
            <semantic:text>Define Pools and lanes (intro) in BPMN terms.
Identify when to apply Pools and lanes (intro).
Model a 4-6 step example using Pools and lanes (intro) correctly.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_ecea09a7-5eca-420e-828a-7999fbe0cda6">
            <semantic:text>Today's concept: Pools and lanes (intro). In a gym membership freeze scenario, ask: What starts the flow, where can it split, how does it rejoin, and what ends it? Name tasks verb-object and avoid crossing lines. If a decision is needed, pick the gateway that matches the semantics. If data crosses pools, use a message flow, not a sequence flow. Keep one clear idea per diagram. Watch the token's journey: make it obvious, and test it with token animation if your tool supports it. Pause and sketch a 5-step example.</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
        <semantic:textAnnotation id="_cde4cd02-6646-4cca-b77a-1c4c396b59f5">
            <semantic:text>include rules for pools and lanes</semantic:text>
        </semantic:textAnnotation>
    </semantic:process>
